  2471'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-112


                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  2472'                         EXADDR:
  2472'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2473'   32 2ACC'              	STA	SRNGF
  2476'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2477'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  2479'   CA 24A2'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  247C'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  247D'   CD 24B7'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2480'   DA 24A0'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2483'   22 2ACD'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2486'   22 2ACF'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  2489'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  248A'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  248C'   C2 2499'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  248F'   13                    	INX	D
  2490'   CD 24B7'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2493'   DA 24A0'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  2496'   22 2ACF'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2499'                         EXA_YS:
  2499'   EB                    	XCHG
  249A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  249C'   32 2ACC'              	STA	SRNGF
  249F'   C9                    	RET
  24A0'                         EXA_NO:
  24A0'   EB                    	XCHG
  24A1'   C9                    	RET
  24A2'                         EXA_AL:
  24A2'   23                    	INX	H
  24A3'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  24A4'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  24A7'   22 2ACD'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  24AA'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  24AD'   22 2ACF'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  24B0'   E1                    	POP	H
  24B1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  24B3'   32 2ACC'              	STA	SRNGF
  24B6'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-113


                                ;digit there at all.
  24B7'                         EXNUM:
  24B7'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  24B8'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  24BA'   D8                    	RC
  24BB'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  24BD'   3F                    	CMC
  24BE'   D8                    	RC
  24BF'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  24C2'                         EXN_LP:
  24C2'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  24C3'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  24C5'   DA 24DF'              	JC	EXN_DN
  24C8'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  24CA'   D2 24DF'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  24CD'   13                    	INX	D
  24CE'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  24CF'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  24D0'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  24D2'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  24D3'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  24D4'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  24D5'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  24D6'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  24D7'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  24D8'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  24D9'   D1                    	POP	D
  24DA'   09                    	DAD	B
  24DB'   C1                    	POP	B
  24DC'   C3 24C2'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  24DF'                         EXN_DN:
  24DF'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  24E0'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  24E1'                         EX_S:
  24E1'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  24E2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24E3'   CA 2525'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  24E6'   32 2AD1'              	STA	SDELIM
  24E9'   23                    	INX	H
  24EA'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  24ED'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  24F0'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  24F1'   CD 252B'              	CALL	SCOPY
  24F4'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24F5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24F6'   CA 2525'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  24F9'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  24FC'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  24FE'   32 2A99'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  2501'   11 2AE3'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  2504'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  2506'   CD 252B'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2509'   78                    	MOV	A,B
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


  250A'   32 2AE2'              	STA	SREPLN
  250D'   AF                    	XRA	A
  250E'   32 2AD2'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2511'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2512'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  2514'   C2 251D'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2517'   23                    	INX	H
  2518'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  251A'   32 2AD2'              	STA	SGFLAG
  251D'                         EX_SG:
  251D'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  251E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  251F'   C2 22BC'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  2522'   C3 254B'              	JMP	SUBGO
  2525'                         SUBNOP:
  2525'   21 2374'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2528'   C3 22CA'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  252B'                         SCOPY:
  252B'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  252D'                         SCP_LP:
  252D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  252E'   B9                    	CMP	C
  252F'   D2 2548'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  2532'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2533'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2534'   CA 2548'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  2537'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2538'   21 2AD1'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  253B'   BE                    	CMP	M
  253C'   E1                    	POP	H
  253D'   CA 2547'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  2540'   12                    	STAX	D
  2541'   13                    	INX	D
  2542'   23                    	INX	H
  2543'   04                    	INR	B
  2544'   C3 252D'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  2547'                         SCP_SK:
  2547'   23                    	INX	H
  2548'                         SCP_DN:
  2548'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2549'   12                    	STAX	D
  254A'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  254B'                         SUBGO:
  254B'   CD 0F83'              	CALL	GZONE
  254E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2551'   22 2AE0'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2554'   21 2ADD'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


  2557'   CD 044F'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  255A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  255B'   32 2AD3'              	STA	SANY
  255E'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2561'   22 2AD5'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2564'   3A 2ACC'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2567'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2568'   CA 257A'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  256B'   CD 1E46'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  256E'   DA 25F6'              	JC	SUBBAD
  2571'   2A 2ACD'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  2574'   CD 0EF1'              	CALL	GOLN
  2577'   C3 2580'              	JMP	SUBLP
  257A'                         SUB_1L:
  257A'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  257D'   22 2AD7'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2580'                         SUBLP:
  2580'   CD 25FC'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2583'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2584'   CA 2592'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  2587'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2589'   32 2AD3'              	STA	SANY
  258C'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  258F'   22 2AD5'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2592'                         SUB_NX:
  2592'   2A 2AD7'              	LHLD	SLINES
  2595'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2596'   22 2AD7'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2599'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  259A'   B5                    	ORA	L
  259B'   CA 25B9'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  259E'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  25A1'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25A2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  25A5'   3A 2AD3'              	LDA	SANY
  25A8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25A9'   CA 25B3'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  25AC'   2A 2AD5'              	LHLD	SBACK
  25AF'   23                    	INX	H
  25B0'   22 2AD5'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  25B3'                         SUB_AE:
  25B3'   CD 0A1D'              	CALL	ATEOF
  25B6'   D2 2580'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  25B9'                         SUBDON:
  25B9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  25BC'   3A 2AD3'              	LDA	SANY
  25BF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25C0'   CA 25DE'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  25C3'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  25C5'   32 2A1A'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-116


  25C8'   2A 2AD5'              	LHLD	SBACK
  25CB'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  25CC'   B5                    	ORA	L
  25CD'   CA 25D8'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  25D0'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  25D2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  25D5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  25D8'                         SUB_PL:
  25D8'   CD 0F9B'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  25DB'   C3 077B'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  25DE'                         SUBNFD:
  25DE'   21 2ADD'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  25E1'   CD 03F6'              	CALL	BACKTO
  25E4'   2A 2AE0'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  25E7'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  25EA'   21 2354'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  25ED'   CD 22CA'              	CALL	EXERR
  25F0'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  25F3'   C3 22C5'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  25F6'                         SUBBAD:
  25F6'   21 2342'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  25F9'   C3 22BF'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  25FC'                         SUBLIN:
  25FC'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25FD'   32 2AD4'              	STA	SLCHG
  2600'   CD 267B'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2603'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  2606'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  2606'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  2609'   DA 2611'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  260C'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  260E'   C2 2606'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  2611'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  2611'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2614'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2615'   CD 14FF'              	CALL	CEND
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-117


  2618'   D1                    	POP	D
  2619'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  261C'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  261D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2620'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2621'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2622'   FA 2674'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  2625'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  2626'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  2627'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  262A'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  262D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2630'   DA 2674'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2633'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  2636'   EB                    	XCHG
  2637'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  263A'   EB                    	XCHG
  263B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  263E'   EB                    	XCHG
  263F'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2640'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2643'   D1                    	POP	D
  2644'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  2645'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  2648'   3A 2AD3'              	LDA	SANY
  264B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  264C'   C2 2659'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  264F'   3A 2AD4'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2652'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2653'   C2 2659'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2656'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  2659'                         SUBL_C:
  2659'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  265C'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  265D'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  265F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2662'   11 2AE2'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  2665'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  2668'                         SUBL_I:
  2668'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  266A'   32 2AD4'              	STA	SLCHG
  266D'   3A 2AD2'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2670'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2671'   C2 2611'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2674'                         SUBL_D:
  2674'   CD 267B'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2677'   3A 2AD4'              	LDA	SLCHG
  267A'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-118


  267B'                         SUBBOL:
  267B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  267E'   CD 05E4'              	CALL	BOL
  2681'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2684'                         VC_SRCH:
  2684'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2687'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2688'   CD 068B'              	CALL	GETCNT
  268B'   22 2AC2'              	SHLD	SCNT
  268E'   C1                    	POP	B
  268F'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2690'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2692'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2694'   CA 2699'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2697'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2699'                         VS_DIR:
  2699'   32 2A97'              	STA	SDIR
  269C'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  269F'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  26A2'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  26A3'   CD 1BE2'              	CALL	RDLINE
  26A6'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26A7'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  26A8'   3A 2A6F'              	LDA	RLLEN
  26AB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26AC'   CA 26C1'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  26AF'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  26B0'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  26B2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  26B5'   19                    	DAD	D
  26B6'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  26B8'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  26B9'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  26BC'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  26BE'   32 2A99'              	STA	SHAVE
  26C1'                         VS_OLD:
  26C1'   3A 2A99'              	LDA	SHAVE
  26C4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26C5'   CA 26D1'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  26C8'   3A 2A97'              	LDA	SDIR
  26CB'   32 2A98'              	STA	SDIRE
  26CE'   C3 26FA'              	JMP	SRCHN
  26D1'                         VS_NOP:
  26D1'   21 2374'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  26D4'   C3 22CA'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  26D7'                         VC_N:
  26D7'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  26DA'   3A 2A97'              	LDA	SDIR
  26DD'   C3 26E7'              	JMP	VN_GO
  26E0'                         VCB_N:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-119


  26E0'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  26E3'   3A 2A97'              	LDA	SDIR
  26E6'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  26E7'                         VN_GO:
  26E7'   32 2A98'              	STA	SDIRE
  26EA'   CD 068B'              	CALL	GETCNT
  26ED'   22 2AC2'              	SHLD	SCNT
  26F0'   3A 2A99'              	LDA	SHAVE
  26F3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26F4'   CA 26D1'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  26F7'   C3 26FA'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  26FA'                         SRCHN:
  26FA'   CD 0F83'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  26FD'   CD 06B4'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and a ^C may stop it
  2700'                         SN_LP:
  2700'   CD 2735'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2703'   DA 2720'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2706'   2A 2AC2'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2709'   2B                    	DCX	H
  270A'   22 2AC2'              	SHLD	SCNT
  270D'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  270E'   B5                    	ORA	L
  270F'   C2 2700'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2712'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2715'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2718'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  271A'   32 2AA4'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  271D'   C3 0F9B'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2720'                         SN_BAD:
  2720'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2723'   DA 0703'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  2726'   CD 06F6'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2729'   21 2354'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  272C'   CD 22CA'              	CALL	EXERR
  272F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2732'   C3 22C5'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2735'                         SRCHGO:
  2735'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2737'   32 2ACB'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  273A'   CD 2750'              	CALL	SRCHW
  273D'   D0                    	RNC
  273E'   CD 06EF'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2741'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2742'   3A 2A98'              	LDA	SDIRE
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-120


  2745'   2F                    	CMA
  2746'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2749'   AF                    	XRA	A
  274A'   32 2ACB'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  274D'   C3 2750'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2750'                         SRCHW:
  2750'   3A 2A98'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2753'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2756'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2759'   22 2A17'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  275C'   AF                    	XRA	A
  275D'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2760'                         SW_LP:
  2760'   CD 06EF'              	CALL	BRKQ		; a ^C spent the count, here or on the way to
  2763'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2764'   CD 2770'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2767'   D0                    	RNC
  2768'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  276B'   D2 2760'              	JNC	SW_LP
  276E'   37                    	STC
  276F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2770'                         SRCH1:
  2770'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2773'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2776'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2777'   FA 279B'              	JM	SR1_BK
  277A'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  277D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2780'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2783'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2784'   3A 2ACB'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2787'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2788'   CA 2791'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  278B'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  278C'   B1                    	ORA	C
  278D'   37                    	STC
  278E'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  278F'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2790'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-121


  2791'                         SR1_FW:
  2791'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2794'   D8                    	RC
  2795'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2798'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  279B'                         SR1_BK:
  279B'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  279E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  27A1'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  27A4'   D8                    	RC
  27A5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  27A8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  27AB'   D8                    	RC
  27AC'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  27AF'                         MSGNAM:
  27AF'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27B0'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  27B2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  27B5'   E1                    	POP	H
  27B6'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  27B7'   23                    	INX	H
  27B8'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27B9'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27BB'   C2 27C8'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  27BE'   21 23E2'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  27C1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  27C4'   E1                    	POP	H
  27C5'   C3 2812'              	JMP	MN_END
  27C8'                         MN_HAS:
  27C8'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  27CA'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  27CD'   E1                    	POP	H
  27CE'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27CF'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


  27D0'   BE                    	CMP	M
  27D1'   CA 27DF'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  27D4'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27D5'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  27D7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27DA'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  27DC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27DF'                         MN_NAM:
  27DF'   E1                    	POP	H
  27E0'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27E1'   23                    	INX	H
  27E2'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  27E4'                         MN_NM:
  27E4'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27E5'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27E7'   CA 27F2'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  27EA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27ED'   23                    	INX	H
  27EE'   0D                    	DCR	C
  27EF'   C2 27E4'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  27F2'                         MN_EXT:
  27F2'   E1                    	POP	H
  27F3'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  27F6'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  27F7'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27F8'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27FA'   CA 2812'              	JZ	MN_END
  27FD'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  27FF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2802'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2804'                         MN_XL:
  2804'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2805'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2807'   CA 2812'              	JZ	MN_END
  280A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  280D'   23                    	INX	H
  280E'   0D                    	DCR	C
  280F'   C2 2804'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2812'                         MN_END:
  2812'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2814'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2817'                         MSGFLG:
  2817'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2819'   B7                    	ORA	A
  281A'   CA 282A'              	JZ	MF_RO
  281D'   3A 2A1A'              	LDA	MODF
  2820'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2821'   CA 282A'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2824'   21 23EC'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2827'   CD 283F'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  282A'                         MF_RO:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


  282A'   3A 2A85'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  282D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  282E'   CA 2837'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2831'   21 23F7'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2834'   CD 283F'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2837'                         MF_NEW:
  2837'   3A 2A83'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  283A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  283B'   C8                    	RZ
  283C'   21 2402'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  283F'                         MF_PUT:
  283F'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2840'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2841'   C2 284D'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2844'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2846'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2847'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2849'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  284C'   E1                    	POP	H
  284D'                         MF_STR:
  284D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2850'                         EMITW:
  2850'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2851'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2852'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2854'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2857'   CD 2875'              	CALL	EWDIG
  285A'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  285D'   CD 2875'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2860'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2863'   CD 2875'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2866'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2869'   CD 2875'              	CALL	EWDIG
  286C'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  286D'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  286F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2872'   D1                    	POP	D
  2873'   C1                    	POP	B
  2874'   C9                    	RET
  2875'                         EWDIG:
  2875'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2877'                         EW_LP:
  2877'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2878'   93                    	SUB	E
  2879'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  287A'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  287B'   9A                    	SBB	D
  287C'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  287D'   DA 2884'              	JC	EW_DN
  2880'   0C                    	INR	C
  2881'   C3 2877'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2884'                         EW_DN:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


  2884'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2885'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2886'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2887'   C2 288D'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  288A'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  288B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  288C'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  288D'                         EW_PR:
  288D'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  288E'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2890'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2893'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2895'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2896'                         ISEMPT:
  2896'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2899'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  289A'   B5                    	ORA	L
  289B'   C0                    	RNZ
  289C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  289F'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  28A0'   B5                    	ORA	L
  28A1'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  28A2'                         MSGCTG:
  28A2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  28A5'   CD 27AF'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  28A8'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  28AA'   CD 2817'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  28AD'   CD 2896'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  28B0'   C2 28B9'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  28B3'   21 2415'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  28B6'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  28B9'                         MG_POS:
  28B9'   21 2408'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  28BC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  28BF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  28C2'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  28C3'   CD 2850'              	CALL	EMITW
  28C6'   21 240F'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  28C9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  28CC'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  28CF'   23                    	INX	H
  28D0'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28D1'   CD 2850'              	CALL	EMITW
  28D4'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  28D7'   23                    	INX	H
  28D8'   D1                    	POP	D
  28D9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  28DC'   C8                    	RZ
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  28DD'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28DE'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  28E0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  28E3'   E1                    	POP	H
  28E4'   C3 2850'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  28E7'                         FILMSG:
  28E7'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  28EA'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  28EC'   C8                    	RZ
  28ED'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  28F0'   CD 27AF'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  28F3'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  28F4'   C3 2817'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  28F7'                         WRMSG:
  28F7'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  28FA'   CD 27AF'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  28FD'   21 2427'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2900'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2903'                         MSGPST:
  2903'   3A 2A82'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2906'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2907'   C8                    	RZ
  2908'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2909'   AF                    	XRA	A
  290A'   32 2A82'              	STA	MSGWHT
  290D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  290E'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2910'   CA 292C'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2913'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2915'   CA 2932'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2918'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  291A'   CA 2923'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  291D'   CD 28A2'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2920'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2923'                         MP_BIG:
  2923'   21 23C3'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  2926'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2929'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  292C'                         MP_FIL:
  292C'   CD 28E7'              	CALL	FILMSG
  292F'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2932'                         MP_WRT:
  2932'   CD 28F7'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2935'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2938'                         VC_CTLG:
  2938'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  293A'   32 2A82'              	STA	MSGWHT
  293D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  293F'   32 2A1B'              	STA	RINTENT
  2942'   C3 068B'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  2945'                         CMDTAB:
  2945'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  2946'   2938'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  2948'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2949'   07B3'                 	DW	VC_H
  294B'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  294C'   07DD'                 	DW	VC_L
  294E'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  294F'   08AE'                 	DW	VC_J
  2951'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  2952'   08C0'                 	DW	VC_K
  2954'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2955'   0810'                 	DW	VC_0
  2957'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2958'   082E'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  295A'   24                    	DB	'$'
  295B'   086C'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  295D'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  295E'   0890'                 	DW	VC_CR
